' Component Notes
note right of ApiIntegrationTestBase
  <b>Test Fixture Pattern</b>

  Base class providing shared test infrastructure.

  <b>Responsibilities:</b>
  • Server lifecycle management (@BeforeAll/@AfterAll)
  • HTTP client configuration
  • Helper methods for HTTP requests
  • Response parsing and assertions

  <b>Key Methods:</b>
  • startServer(): Starts embedded Tomcat on test port
  • stopServer(): Gracefully shuts down server
  • get/post/put/delete(): HTTP request helpers
  • assertStatus(): Response validation

  <b>Benefits:</b>
  • DRY: Shared setup across all test classes
  • Consistent test environment
  • Simplified test writing
end note

note bottom of DealServletIntegrationTest
  <b>Concrete Test Class</b>

  Tests all DealServlet endpoints end-to-end.

  <b>Test Naming Convention:</b>
  methodName_scenario_expectedResult

  Examples:
  • createDeal_validData_returns201
  • getDealById_nonExistentId_returns404

  <b>AAA Pattern:</b>
  • Arrange: Set up test data
  • Act: Make HTTP request
  • Assert: Verify response

  <b>Test Coverage:</b>
  • Happy path (200, 201, 204)
  • Error cases (400, 404)
  • Filtering and querying
  • Full CRUD cycle
end note

note bottom of SampleDataLoader
  <b>Test Data Builder Pattern</b>

  Generates realistic test data for demos and testing.

  <b>Features:</b>
  • Creates users with different roles
  • Generates deals in various states
  • Sets up commission plans
  • Creates sample disputes

  <b>Usage:</b>
  Can be invoked from:
  • ApiServer.main() with --load-sample-data flag
  • Test setup methods
  • Manual data population

  <b>Data Characteristics:</b>
  • Realistic business scenarios
  • Diverse entity states
  • Relational consistency
end note

note left of ApiServer
  <b>System Under Test</b>

  The actual REST API server being tested.

  <b>Test Configuration:</b>
  • Runs on separate port (9999)
  • Isolated from production
  • Fresh instance per test class
  • In-memory repositories

  <b>Lifecycle:</b>
  1. @BeforeAll: Server started
  2. Tests execute
  3. @AfterAll: Server stopped

  <b>Thread Model:</b>
  Server runs in background thread
  to avoid blocking test execution.
end note

note bottom of HttpClient
  <b>Java HTTP Client (JDK 11+)</b>

  Built-in HTTP client for making requests.

  <b>Advantages over libraries:</b>
  • No external dependencies
  • Type-safe request builders
  • Async support
  • HTTP/2 support

  <b>Usage in Tests:</b>
  httpClient.send(
      HttpRequest.newBuilder()
          .uri(URI.create(url))
          .POST(BodyPublishers.ofString(json))
          .build(),
      BodyHandlers.ofString()
  )
end note

note right of DealServlet
  <b>Servlet Under Test</b>

  Integration tests verify:
  • HTTP routing (GET /deals)
  • Request parsing (JSON → Object)
  • Business logic invocation
  • Response generation (Object → JSON)
  • Status code accuracy
  • Error handling

  Unlike unit tests, integration tests
  exercise the full request/response cycle
  through the actual HTTP stack.
end note

' Legend
legend right
  <b>Integration Testing Concepts:</b>

  <b>Test Fixture:</b> Shared setup and teardown
  for a group of tests. Provides common
  infrastructure like server, client, helpers.

  <b>Integration Test:</b> Tests multiple components
  working together. Here: HTTP client → Servlet
  → Repository → JSON serialization.

  <b>AAA Pattern:</b>
  • Arrange: Set up test conditions
  • Act: Execute the operation
  • Assert: Verify expected outcome

  <b>Test Data Builder:</b> Pattern for creating
  complex test objects with realistic data.

  <b>Test Lifecycle Annotations:</b>
  • @BeforeAll: Runs once before all tests
  • @AfterAll: Runs once after all tests
  • @Test: Marks a test method
  • @DisplayName: Readable test description
  • @Order: Control test execution order

  <b>HTTP Testing:</b>
  Integration tests use real HTTP requests
  over localhost. Verifies:
  • Routing and URL mapping
  • Request/response serialization
  • HTTP status codes
  • Header handling
  • Error responses

  <b>Test Isolation:</b>
  Each test class gets fresh server instance.
  Tests use separate port from production.
  In-memory storage ensures no persistence.

  <b>End-to-End vs Integration:</b>
  • E2E: Test through UI/external client
  • Integration: Test server components together
  • Unit: Test single class in isolation

  Our tests are integration tests: they test
  servlet + repository + JSON together, but
  use test infrastructure (test port, in-memory DB).
endlegend

